When a demo output is a dense repeating field but other examples look unlike it, neither force a shared rule from visual similarity nor dismiss the periodic case as unrelated. Treat the pattern as a testable abstraction: record the output's row/column period, color cycle ordering, direction, and phase/origin; compare these with the input's sparse diagonal tracks, marker sequence, or stripe ordering. Distinguish period from phase (a correct cycle with wrong origin still fails exact replay).

Replay the proposed generator against every complete demo. Include cases where the output is uniform or striped: these may be degenerate instances of a shared periodic generator, or evidence against it, and must not be ignored. A test input that visually resembles a periodic encoding only motivates the hypothesis; it does not establish mapping. If no deterministic cycle/phase rule reproduces all outputs, label it unresolved rather than submitting cosmetic rotations/reversals or repeatedly requesting demos without a discriminating prediction.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-2/play/users/u089/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/periodic-field-cross-demo-test.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/session-task-7db5e3d5-review.md", "content": "# Review: heterogeneous demonstrations without a validated common rule

This instance supplied three sharply different pairs: a 21×21 sparse diagonal-color scene became a full 5-color periodic field; a 23×23 otherwise blank grid with two bottom stripes became an alternating two-color row pattern; and a 2×2 near-uniform patch had its lone zero filled. The 12×12 test instead contained offset diagonal tracks in colors 8, 2, 6, and 7. The demonstrations were evidence of radically different surface transformations, but no cumulative exact-delta analysis produced a validated shared selector or output mapping. Serial demo requests (three were used) did not resolve the relationship; the last action continued requesting rather than making an evidence-based decision.

Reusable lesson: task IDs and stream membership alone do not justify assuming a common pixel-level operation. Record each pair's exact delta and output structure before requesting another. Explicitly test whether the task may be an abstract transformation shared across different visual classes; formulate at least two candidate abstractions and derive predictions that can be replayed against all examples. If no candidate maps all demonstrations exactly, another request is justified only if its predicted outcomes discriminate those candidates. Do not invent a test output by blending the demo patterns or select one demo's operation based on superficial resemblance. Keep the action protocol strict: one JSON action only on the final line.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-2/play/users/u089/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/session-task-7db5e3d5-review.md"}

